await cmd('!drone');
await sleep(150);
check('!drone adds two more', await ev(`window.ARENA.drones.length`) === 8,
  'drones=' + await ev(`window.ARENA.drones.length`));

// ---- extra life powerup
await cmd('!shield');
await sleep(100);
check('shield powerup armed', await ev(`window.ARENA.S.shield`) === true);

// ---- magnet pull changes velocity
await cmd('!magnet');
await sleep(100);
check('magnet timer set', await ev(`window.ARENA.S.magnet`) > 5, 'magnet=' + await ev(`window.ARENA.S.magnet`));

const exc = events.filter(e => e.method === 'Runtime.exceptionThrown');
check('no runtime exceptions', exc.length === 0, exc.length ? JSON.stringify(exc[0]).slice(0, 200) : '');
console.log(results.join('\n'));
console.log(exc.length ? 'EXC ' + JSON.stringify(exc.slice(0, 2)).slice(0, 600) : 'EXC none');
ws.close(); process.exit(0);

parts.text:
Several failures. Let's analyze:

1. "rock splits into two children [14 ...]": clearRocks() was called, but then `A.clearRocks()` removes all rocks... but the count shows 14 right after. Because rocks array had 14 entries? Wait, I called clearRocks() at start, then spawned 1 rock — but the auto-advance logic: rocks.length===0 → nextLevel() → spawnWave() immediately in the same frame! So clearRocks instantly triggers a new wave. That's why count is 14 (wave for level 2 = 9+4=13, plus 1 spawned = 14). So my "spawn in front and fire" test spawned a rock but 13 others also existed. Then firing once killed... unclear.

So the empty-field auto-advance makes precise tests hard. I should make tests robust: count depth-1 rocks before/after.

2. "depth-2 rock vanishes": count=1 after 1.5s — other rocks from the wave. Bad test setup.

3. "multiplier increases": x1 — because killing 5 rocks only decrements killsToNext by 5 (from 14) — not enough for mult 2. My test was wrong: need 14 kills. Also firing 12 times with 5 rocks... Let me instead set killsToNext=2 then kill one rock, and check mult becomes 2.

4. "weakpoint hit deals 6 damage": hp went 274 → 273 (1 damage). Because the bullet I placed at the weakpoint position: updateBullets runs BEFORE updateWeakpoints in the frame. In updateBullets, the bullet checks collision with the boss body (distance < 6.2) → damageBoss(1) and kills the bullet. So the weakpoint check never happens. That's a real design flaw: bullets hitting the boss body deal damage, so weakpoints are pointless — the body absorbs everything.

Fix: in updateBullets, skip the body-collision damage when the bullet is within a weakpoint radius... but the bullet is killed by the body check before. Better approach: check weakpoint proximity in updateBullets too, or reorganize: check weakpoints first in the frame, then body. Simplest robust fix: in updateBullets, when a bullet is within boss radius, first test weakpoint hit (distance < 1.1 to any weakpoint): if yes → damageBoss(6); else damageBoss(1). And keep updateWeakpoints as a redundant safety (or remove it).

Let me restructure: create function `bossBulletHit(bl)` that tests weakpoints then body, doing the appropriate damage and killing the bullet. Call it from updateBullets. Remove updateWeakpoints call in animate (or keep but it becomes no-op since bullets killed). I'll fold weakpoint logic into one place.

Also the "boss dies at 0 hp" check returned boss=true meaning `A.S.boss===null` evaluated true — fine (test asserted true).

5. Also need to re-verify "rock splits" properly: set killsToNext low, spawn one rock in front, wait for auto-advance to create a wave, then count depth-1 rocks before and after a kill.

Actually the auto-advance on empty field is a design decision: when all rocks cleared → next level. That's fine but makes waves churn. Keep.

Let me now fix the code: boss bullet handling with weakpoint priority.

Implementation in updateBullets:
```js
if(S.boss&&b.mesh.position.distanceTo(S.boss.mesh.position)<6.2){
  bossHit(b);
  break; // or return
}
```
where
```js
function bossHit(b){
  const bo=S.boss;
  let weak=false;
  for(const w of bo.weak){
    if(b.mesh.position.distanceTo(w.mesh.position)<1.4){weak=true;break;}
  }
  damageBoss(weak?6:1);
  FX.burst(b.mesh.position, weak?26:8, new THREE.Color(weak?0xffe08a:0xffd23f), weak?9:6, weak?0.9:0.6, weak?0.6:0.35);
  if(weak){bo.vulnerable=2.0;feed('weakpoint hit','ok');}
  bullets.kill(b);
}
```
Note: after damageBoss, the boss may be dead (killBoss → S.boss=null, nextLevel). Careful: `bo` reference still valid for FX. But if boss died, bo.weak meshes removed from scene — FX uses positions only, fine. Also if S.boss becomes null, we should not use S.boss afterwards. In bossHit we capture bo first. Good.

Also updateWeakpoints function becomes unnecessary; I'll remove it and its call.

Also: bullets hitting boss body should deal 1 damage but maybe make body hit deal 1 and weakpoint 6 — as designed. Good.

Additionally: should enemy (boss) bullets be blocked by weakpoints? No.

Let me apply the patch, plus re-verify with a corrected test.

Another thing to check: `registerKill` spawns powerups with 9% chance; fine.

Also check: in updateBullets the boss body check happens AFTER rock loop with `if(!b.alive)return;` — good.

Let me patch.

